\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

The results establish three insights that frame CIVO as a structural vulnerability of flat temporal conditioning in imagination-based RL. Full mechanistic analysis, gradient decomposition, and seed-divergence discussion are in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_analysis}.

\paragraph{CIVO mechanism.}
The RSSM reward head develops a $2.78\times$ prediction gap between $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$ under trained conditioning (Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}), providing the systematic bias that the critic internalizes and the actor exploits---a four-stage feedback loop analogous to delusional bias \citep{lu2018nondelusional} but model-induced rather than policy-class-induced.  Full-pathway SGS (exp\_018) targets the gradient channel that maintains this bias, yet yields mixed results (50\% collapse prevention), indicating that CIVO involves mechanisms beyond gradient-mediated co-adaptation of the temporal embedding.

\paragraph{Gradient decomposition and intervention design.}
\label{sec:gradient_evolution_main}
Gradient decomposition (Figure~\ref{fig:gradient_decomp}, Table~\ref{tab:gradient_decomposition}) reveals a non-obvious structure: the reward head provides the largest gradient contribution to $e_{\Delta t}$ (49.0\% at 55K, 38.1\% at 112K), yet reward-only SGS (exp\_016e) is functionally insufficient (NVE $10.42\times$, collapse).  The observation decoder channel contributes the smallest share (16.3\% at 55K, 21.5\% at 112K) but encodes the critical $\dt$-specific spatial predictions that generate the RSSM fidelity gap.  Full-pathway SGS, which removes observation decoder \emph{and} reward gradients from $e_{\Delta t}$, \sgsclaim{} (seed~1: peak NVE $\sgsnve{}\times$, eval \sgseval{}; seed~2: collapsed, eval 40.8).  The 50\% prevention rate confirms that the observation decoder gradient pathway is causally involved but reveals that CIVO's feedback loop has redundant mechanisms not fully captured by gradient decomposition.

\paragraph{Architectural contrast with MPC.}
$\dt$-conditioning succeeds in MPC \citep{nhu2025tawm} because re-planning from real states prevents error accumulation through the critic.  In imagination-based training, the closed actor-critic loop amplifies any systematic reward-prediction bias---the structural factor that transforms manageable model imprecision into pathological value dynamics.  Multiple vulnerability pathways exist: CIVO (Pathway~A, value-driven, entropy decreasing) and non-CIVO instability (Pathway~B, eval-driven, entropy increasing, \S\ref{sec:f6}), suggesting that mitigation strategies targeting the imagination channel alone may be insufficient.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We have presented a systematic factorial diagnostic of temporal abstraction in DreamerV3 across two continuous-control environments, identifying the combination of trained $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure as the causal factor behind conditioning-induced value overestimation (CIVO). The key findings are:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item Every variable-rate imagination configuration degrades relative to the $\dt{=}1$ baseline, with degradation scaling monotonically with $\dt$.
\item A normalized value-to-eval threshold of $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ (35K+ window) achieves 7/8 CIVO classification ($n{=}8$, 87.5\%), with the single false positive arising from a mechanistically distinct non-CIVO collapse. The combination of trained $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure is the causal factor; unexposed, frozen, or removed embeddings prevent overestimation.
\item The RSSM reward head develops a $2.78\times$ prediction gap between $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$ under trained conditioning, providing the systematic bias that drives critic overestimation.
\item The same CIVO trigger produces stochastic downstream outcomes---full collapse and transient CIVO with self-correction---across seeds of identical configurations.
\item Restricting imagination to $\dt{=}1$ does not fully prevent collapse: one of two seeds fails via a qualitatively distinct non-CIVO pathway (eval-driven with increasing entropy), demonstrating that the trained $\dt$-embedding creates vulnerability through multiple mechanisms.
\end{enumerate}

The contrast with TAWM's success in MPC identifies the feedback architecture as the critical factor: $\dt$-conditioning succeeds when errors are contained by re-planning from real states, but fails when errors compound through imagination-based actor-critic bootstrapping.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
Our primary factorial analysis focuses on Walker Walk; cross-environment evidence (Cheetah Run) is limited to a single seed. Both use a single world-model architecture (DreamerV3/RSSM). Generalization to other environments and architectures---particularly transformer-based world models such as DreamerV4---remains an open question. The $\nve$ threshold ($1.5\times$, 35K+ window) is sensitive to value overestimation broadly but not perfectly specific to CIVO (7/8, one non-CIVO false positive; margin $0.62$ excluding the outlier); generalization to other tasks and architectures is pending validation.

\paragraph{Future directions.}
Our findings suggest three avenues. First, hierarchical temporal abstraction at the representation level---as in THICK and Hieros---may avoid the flat-conditioning failure mode by maintaining separate dynamics for each temporal scale. Second, gradient decomposition reveals that gradient importance is decoupled from magnitude: the reward head provides the largest share (38--49\%) but reward-only SGS fails, while the observation decoder channel (16--22\%) carries the critical $\dt$-specific information driving the fidelity gap. Full-pathway gradient stopping \sgsclaim{} (seed~1 eval \sgseval{}, seed~2 collapsed), indicating that cutting the observation decoder gradient pathway is necessary but not sufficient; the 50\% prevention rate points to redundant mechanisms in CIVO's feedback loop that future interventions must address jointly. Third, extending this diagnostic framework to transformer-based world models and continuous-control benchmarks would clarify whether CIVO is RSSM-specific or a broader vulnerability of temporal conditioning in imagination-based RL.

